@page "/blogs/distributed-testing-with-aspire-and-playwright-in-dotnet"
@using SharedModels
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(DistributedTestingWithAspireAndPlaywrightInDotNet)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                An operator can generate an order successfully and the owner can still open an
                empty dashboard. A passing API test won't tell you whether both users can finish
                their part of the job.
            </p>
            <p>
                <Highlight>Aspire.Hosting.Testing</Highlight> starts the services defined by your
                AppHost, the project that describes how they run together.
                <Highlight>Playwright</Highlight> drives the browser. We'll use them to follow
                one cash order from a new branch to the owner's dashboard.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                An order endpoint test can tell you whether the API accepts a valid request.
                It won't catch a login redirect that drops the operator at the wrong screen
                or a dashboard that reads orders for the wrong branch.
            </p>
            <p>
                Keep the focused tests for service rules: they're cheaper to run and narrow
                down failures faster. Save browser tests for critical handoffs like this one:
                the operator records the sale, and the owner relies on it to track the day.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>Follow one order across three roles</h4>
            <p>
                An admin creates an isolated client and branch with an owner and an operator.
                The owner configures the branch, adds a category, item, and supplier, then
                records a purchase to stock the item. The operator creates a customer and
                generates a cash order; the owner signs back in to check the dashboard.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-plaintext">
xUnit assembly fixture --&gt; AppHost --&gt; landing (health probe)
Playwright browser --&gt; web --&gt; api --&gt; PostgreSQL (application DB)
                        \--&gt; identity --&gt; PostgreSQL (identity DB)
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The test uses the same service names as the
                <a class="underline" href="/blogs/getting-started-with-aspire-in-dotnet/">companion AppHost walkthrough</a>.
                This map shows the main browser path and the fixture's landing probe, not every
                AppHost dependency.
            </p>

            <h4>Generate a cash order and check both screens</h4>
            <p>
                Use xUnit v3, <Highlight>Aspire.Hosting.Testing</Highlight>, and
                <Highlight>Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit.v3</Highlight> in a distributed-test
                project that references your AppHost. These excerpts leave admin and branch
                setup, sign-in, and cleanup to your application.
            </p>
            <p>
                <Highlight>CreateBranchAndUsersAsync</Highlight> signs in as an admin and
                returns the branch and user details along with unique test data for the
                customer and item. <Highlight>SignInAsAsync</Highlight> replaces the current
                login using your app's test configuration. Match the locators to your screens'
                accessible names.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Aspire.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Playwright;
using Xunit;

public sealed partial class E2ETests(AppFixture fixture) : DistributedPageTest
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task Cash_order_appears_in_orders_and_owner_dashboard()
    {
        var runId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
        await Page.GotoAsync(fixture.App.GetEndpoint("web", "https").ToString());
        try
        {
            var branch = await CreateBranchAndUsersAsync(runId);
            await SignInAsAsync(branch.Owner);
            await StockItemThroughUiAsync(branch);
            await SignInAsAsync(branch.Operator);
            await CreateCustomerAsync(branch.CustomerName, branch.CustomerMobile);
            await GenerateCashOrderAsync(branch.CustomerName, branch.CustomerMobile,
                branch.CategoryName, branch.ItemName);

            await Page.ReloadAsync();
            await Expect(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Row)
                .Filter(new() { HasText = branch.CustomerName })).ToBeVisibleAsync();

            await SignInAsAsync(branch.Owner);
            await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Link, new() { Name = "Dashboard" }).ClickAsync();
            await Expect(WidgetValue("Today's Orders")).ToHaveTextAsync("1");
            await Expect(WidgetValue("This Month's Top Selling"))
                .ToHaveTextAsync(branch.ItemName);
        }
        finally
        {
            await RemoveBranchScenarioAsync(runId);
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The customer you save is the customer you select on the order form. Add these
                helpers to the same test class. The dashboard helper finds a card by its heading
                and reads its value; adjust the card and value selectors to your markup.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
private async Task CreateCustomerAsync(string name, string mobile)
{
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Link, new() { Name = "Customer" }).ClickAsync();
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "New Customer" }).ClickAsync();
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Textbox, new() { Name = "First Name" }).FillAsync(name);
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Textbox, new() { Name = "Mobile Number" }).FillAsync(mobile);
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Save changes" }).ClickAsync();
    await Expect(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Row)
        .Filter(new() { HasText = name })).ToBeVisibleAsync();
}

private async Task GenerateCashOrderAsync(
    string customer, string mobile, string category, string item)
{
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Link, new() { Name = "Order", Exact = true })
        .ClickAsync();
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Searchbox,
        new() { Name = "type customer name or mobile" }).FillAsync(mobile[..3]);
    await Page.GetByText($"{customer} ({mobile})", new() { Exact = true }).ClickAsync();
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Cell, new() { Name = category, Exact = true })
        .ClickAsync();
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Cell, new() { Name = item, Exact = true })
        .ClickAsync();
    await Page.GetByLabel("Payment Mode").SelectOptionAsync(["Cash"]);
    await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Generate" }).ClickAsync();
    await Expect(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Row)
        .Filter(new() { HasText = customer })).ToBeVisibleAsync();
}

private ILocator WidgetValue(string title) =&gt;
    Page.Locator("div.card")
        .Filter(new()
        {
            Has = Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Heading, new() { Name = title, Exact = true })
        })
        .Locator("p.text-xl");
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Share the AppHost, not the browser session</h4>
            <p>
                xUnit injects one <Highlight>AppFixture</Highlight> into the test classes that
                need it. Playwright's <Highlight>PageTest</Highlight> gives each test a fresh
                browser context, so cookies and local storage don't carry over. The database
                <em>does</em> carry over between tests in the assembly, which is why the order test
                uses unique records and cleans up its own branch.
            </p>
            <p>
                Start with a disposable PostgreSQL instance for each run. Your application's
                migrations and seed code prepare the schema and baseline data; Aspire doesn't
                supply the seed data. That's how you get a useful starting point
                without filling a shared staging database with test orders.
            </p>
            <p>
                Put the following fixture excerpts in one class, using your generated AppHost
                type in place of <Highlight>Projects.AppHost</Highlight>. A startup timeout
                names the stage that stalled, so you know which service to investigate before
                looking for a browser failure.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Aspire.Hosting;
using Aspire.Hosting.Testing;
using Xunit;

[assembly: CaptureConsole]
[assembly: AssemblyFixture(typeof(AppFixture))]

public sealed class AppFixture : IAsyncLifetime
{
    private static readonly TimeSpan StartupLimit =
        TimeSpan.FromSeconds(120);
    private static readonly TimeSpan ShutdownLimit =
        TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30);
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _shutdown = new();
    private IDistributedApplicationTestingBuilder? _builder;
    private DistributedApplication? _app;
    private bool _disposed;

    public DistributedApplication App =&gt;
        _app ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("AppHost is not ready.");
            </CodeSnippet>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    public async ValueTask InitializeAsync()
    {
        var testCancellation = TestContext.Current.CancellationToken;
        using var startup = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(
            _shutdown.Token, testCancellation);
        startup.CancelAfter(StartupLimit);
        var stage = "create AppHost";
        try
        {
            _builder = await DistributedApplicationTestingBuilder
                .CreateAsync&lt;Projects.AppHost&gt;(startup.Token);
            stage = "build AppHost";
            _app = await _builder.BuildAsync(startup.Token);
            stage = "start AppHost";
            await _app.StartAsync(startup.Token);
            stage = "landing health";
            await App.ResourceNotifications.WaitForResourceHealthyAsync(
                "landing", startup.Token);
            stage = "identity discovery";
            using var identity = App.CreateHttpClient("identity", "https");
            await WaitForSuccessAsync(
                identity, "/.well-known/openid-configuration", startup.Token);
            stage = "API ping";
            using var api = App.CreateHttpClient("api", "https");
            await WaitForSuccessAsync(api, "/api/ping", startup.Token);
        }
        catch (Exception startupError)
        {
            Exception failure = startupError;
            if (startupError is OperationCanceledException &&
                startup.IsCancellationRequested && !testCancellation.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                failure = new TimeoutException($"AppHost timed out at: {stage}.", startupError);
            }
            try
            {
                await DisposeAsync();
            }
            catch (Exception cleanupError)
            {
                throw new AggregateException(
                    "AppHost startup and cleanup both failed.",
                    failure, cleanupError);
            }
            if (failure != startupError) throw failure;
            throw;
        }
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                One two-minute deadline covers startup and all three readiness checks. The
                probe retries connection and server errors. If your client uses a resilience
                timeout, also treat its <Highlight>TimeoutRejectedException</Highlight> as
                retryable within that same deadline.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    private static async Task WaitForSuccessAsync(
        HttpClient client, string path, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        while (true)
        {
            try
            {
                using var response = await client.GetAsync(path, cancellationToken);
                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
                return;
            }
            catch (HttpRequestException ex) when (
                ex.StatusCode is null || (int)ex.StatusCode.Value &gt;= 500)
            {
                await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500), cancellationToken);
            }
        }
    }
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The fixture owns environment teardown, even if startup fails before the host
                exists. Stop the host with a separate deadline so a canceled startup token
                doesn't cancel cleanup.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        if (_disposed) return;
        _disposed = true;
        _shutdown.Cancel();
        using var shutdown = new CancellationTokenSource(ShutdownLimit);
        try
        {
            if (_app is not null)
            {
                try { await _app.StopAsync(shutdown.Token); }
                finally { await _app.DisposeAsync(); }
            }
            else if (_builder is not null)
            {
                await _builder.DisposeAsync();
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _shutdown.Dispose();
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Save a recording you can match to the test</h4>
            <p>
                When the dashboard check fails, the assertion tells you which value was wrong.
                The recording shows how the browser got there: for example, which branch the
                owner was viewing. Keep the timeouts in the shared base class and opt in to
                video in <Highlight>E2ETests</Highlight> before Playwright creates the context.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Playwright;
using Microsoft.Playwright.Xunit.v3;

public abstract class DistributedPageTest : PageTest
{
    public override async ValueTask InitializeAsync()
    {
        await base.InitializeAsync();
        Assertions.SetDefaultExpectTimeout(30_000);
        Context.SetDefaultTimeout(60_000);
        Context.SetDefaultNavigationTimeout(60_000);
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Close the context to finish the video, then save it with the test name. The
                suffix keeps repeat runs from overwriting each other, and the original goes
                away only after the named copy is saved.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Playwright;
using Xunit;

public sealed partial class E2ETests
{
    private static readonly string VideoDirectory = Path.GetFullPath("videos/e2e-tests");

    public override BrowserNewContextOptions ContextOptions() =&gt; new()
    {
        RecordVideoDir = VideoDirectory,
        RecordVideoSize = new() { Width = 1280, Height = 720 },
        ViewportSize = new() { Width = 1280, Height = 720 }
    };

    public override async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            var video = Page?.Video;
            if (video is null) return;
            var displayName = TestContext.Current.Test!.TestDisplayName;

            await Context.CloseAsync();
            var invalid = Path.GetInvalidFileNameChars();
            var testName = string.Concat(displayName.Split('.').Last()
                .Select(c =&gt; invalid.Contains(c) ? '_' : c));
            await video.SaveAsAsync(Path.Combine(
                VideoDirectory, $"{testName}-{Guid.NewGuid():N}.webm"));
            await video.DeleteAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            await base.DisposeAsync();
        }
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Run the test and read the evidence</h4>
            <p>
                From your distributed-test project's directory, run:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-powershell">
dotnet test
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The Orders row after a reload checks that the operator can still find the saved
                order. In a new branch, <strong>Today's Orders = 1</strong> checks that
                the order reached the owner's dashboard, and <strong>This Month's Top Selling</strong>
                ties that result to the item the owner stocked. Playwright's retrying
                <Highlight>Expect</Highlight> assertions wait for those values within the
                configured timeout, without guessing how long the page takes to update.
            </p>
            <p>
                On a failed run, start with the assertion and captured console output. If a
                recording was saved, look in <Highlight>videos/e2e-tests</Highlight> under the
                test process's working directory for the file starting with the test name.
            </p>
            <p>
                Give a coding agent that same loop: implement the change, run
                <Highlight>dotnet test</Highlight>, read the failing assertion and output,
                fix the issue, then rerun the test. Ask it to hand over the named recording
                so you can review the flow yourself.
            </p>

            <h4>Extend the same test across teams</h4>
            <p>
                In a larger system, your AppHost can run another team's published container
                alongside your local projects. Pin the image to a version or digest so you know
                which contract you're testing.
            </p>
            <p>
                If that workflow uses messaging, wait for both the broker and the consumer
                to be ready before starting the journey. Match the downstream effect to the
                unique order or event ID, then assert the final state in the UI. A broker is
                an optional extension, not part of this order example. Use test-owned brokers
                and data, just as with PostgreSQL.
            </p>

